{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Fv19zDkFwVqFwadw2JiWd8","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"history of timekeeping sundial to atomic clock\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"The History of Clocks: From Sundials to Atomic Timekeeping | Cloudnola\",\"url\":\"https://cloudnola.com/blogs/news/the-history-of-clocks-from-sundials-to-atomic-timekeeping\"},{\"title\":\"History of the Clock: From Sundials to Atomic Timekeeping | MFPS\",\"url\":\"https://www.myfrontpagestory.com/history-of-the-clock/\"},{\"title\":\"From Sundials To Atomic Clocks: Evolution Of Timekeeping Throughout History - PWOnlyIAS\",\"url\":\"https://pwonlyias.com/current-affairs/from-sundials-to-atomic-clocks/\"},{\"title\":\"History of Horology: Sundials to Atomic Clocks | Galleon Systems Ltd\",\"url\":\"https://www.galsys.co.uk/time-reference/history-of-timekeeping/history-of-horology.html\"},{\"title\":\"A brief history of timekeeping – Physics World\",\"url\":\"https://physicsworld.com/a/a-brief-history-of-timekeeping/\"},{\"title\":\"History of Timekeeping\",\"url\":\"https://www.timeanddate.com/time/history-of-timekeeping.html\"},{\"title\":\"Sundial to the Atomic Clock | Resonance\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s12045-022-1390-6\"},{\"title\":\"Measurement of Time - From Sundial, Hourglass to Atomic Clock\",\"url\":\"https://testbook.com/physics/what-is-time-atomic-clock\"},{\"title\":\"The History of Timekeeping: From Ancient Sundials to Atomic Clocks\",\"url\":\"https://timernow.com/history-of-timekeeping/\"},{\"title\":\"A Journey Through Time: The Fascinating History of Clocks and Horology – Hermle North America\",\"url\":\"https://hermleclock.com/blogs/news/a-journey-through-time-the-fascinating-history-of-clocks-and-horology\"}]\n\nHere's an overview of the history of timekeeping from sundials to atomic clocks:\n\n## Early Timekeeping Devices\n\n### Sundials\nThe sundial was one of the earliest known timekeeping devices and was used by the ancient Egyptians around 1500 BCE.(no content)Sundials can trace their origin back over five thousand years, when obelisks were built, possibly to allow the telling of time by the cast of their shadows.(no content)Sundials were made up of a dial and a gnomon, which cast a shadow on the dial that indicated the time of day based on the position of the sun.\n\n### Water Clocks\nThe water clock, also known as the clepsydra, was invented by the ancient Greeks around 500 BCE.(no content)Water clocks relied on the steady flow of water to measure time. By filling a container with water, the water level would drop over time, allowing users to track the passage of time.(no content)Unlike sundials, they could function at night and during cloudy weather.\n\n## Mechanical Clocks\n\nMechanical clocks first appeared in the 14th century, which were driven by weight and regulated by a verge-and-foliot escapement (a system that advanced the gear train at regular intervals or 'ticks').(no content)Timekeeping took a major step forward in 1283, when the first weight-driven mechanical clock was set in motion in England.\n\n### Pendulum Clocks\nThe invention of the pendulum clock in the 17th century marked another significant advancement in horology. It was Dutch mathematician and scientist Christiaan Huygens who successfully implemented the idea in 1656.(no content)Pendulum clocks were far more accurate than any preceding timekeeping devices.\n\n## Modern Timekeeping\n\n### Quartz Clocks\nThe first quartz clock was built in 1927 by Warren Marrison and Joseph Horton.(no content)These were based on the movement of a vibrating crystal (usually quartz) to create an electric signal with an exact frequency.(no content)Quartz clocks are inexpensive to make and easy to operate, and their invention led to watches and wall-clocks becoming very common from the mid-20th century.\n\n### Atomic Clocks\nIn 1955, NPL set in motion a revolution in timekeeping when Louis Essen and Jack Parry produced the first practical caesium atomic frequency standard.(no content)Atomic clocks use the vibrations of atoms, typically cesium or rubidium, to measure time with extraordinary precision.(no content)Most atomic clocks use the resonance of the atom caesium-133 which vibrates exactly at a frequency of 9,192,631,770 every second. Since 1967 the International System of Units (SI) has defined the second as that number of cycles from this atom.(no content)An atomic clock is accurate to within one second every 100 million years.(no content)These clocks are now the gold standard in timekeeping, and they are used in everything from GPS satellites to scientific research.\n\n## The Future: Optical Clocks\nThe latest innovations, like optical lattice clocks, are outperforming even atomic clocks. Some of these new timers are expected to become mind-bogglingly accurate, losing only one second every 30 billion years.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2854a34c-731f-4835-93ff-d255d2f6ad4c","uuid":"f0cfdb80-9b41-42da-b03d-bd825d01d641","tool_use_result":{"query":"history of timekeeping sundial to atomic clock","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01GdEsxvrde59Q71opTAcRtW","content":[{"title":"The History of Clocks: From Sundials to Atomic Timekeeping | Cloudnola","url":"https://cloudnola.com/blogs/news/the-history-of-clocks-from-sundials-to-atomic-timekeeping"},{"title":"History of the Clock: From Sundials to Atomic Timekeeping | MFPS","url":"https://www.myfrontpagestory.com/history-of-the-clock/"},{"title":"From Sundials To Atomic Clocks: Evolution Of Timekeeping Throughout History - PWOnlyIAS","url":"https://pwonlyias.com/current-affairs/from-sundials-to-atomic-clocks/"},{"title":"History of Horology: Sundials to Atomic Clocks | Galleon Systems Ltd","url":"https://www.galsys.co.uk/time-reference/history-of-timekeeping/history-of-horology.html"},{"title":"A brief history of timekeeping – Physics World","url":"https://physicsworld.com/a/a-brief-history-of-timekeeping/"},{"title":"History of Timekeeping","url":"https://www.timeanddate.com/time/history-of-timekeeping.html"},{"title":"Sundial to the Atomic Clock | Resonance","url":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s12045-022-1390-6"},{"title":"Measurement of Time - From Sundial, Hourglass to Atomic Clock","url":"https://testbook.com/physics/what-is-time-atomic-clock"},{"title":"The History of Timekeeping: From Ancient Sundials to Atomic Clocks","url":"https://timernow.com/history-of-timekeeping/"},{"title":"A Journey Through Time: The Fascinating History of Clocks and Horology – Hermle North America","url":"https://hermleclock.com/blogs/news/a-journey-through-time-the-fascinating-history-of-clocks-and-horology"}]},"Here's an overview of the history of timekeeping from sundials to atomic clocks:\n\n## Early Timekeeping Devices\n\n### Sundials\nThe sundial was one of the earliest known timekeeping devices and was used by the ancient Egyptians around 1500 BCE.(no content)Sundials can trace their origin back over five thousand years, when obelisks were built, possibly to allow the telling of time by the cast of their shadows.(no content)Sundials were made up of a dial and a gnomon, which cast a shadow on the dial that indicated the time of day based on the position of the sun.\n\n### Water Clocks\nThe water clock, also known as the clepsydra, was invented by the ancient Greeks around 500 BCE.(no content)Water clocks relied on the steady flow of water to measure time. By filling a container with water, the water level would drop over time, allowing users to track the passage of time.(no content)Unlike sundials, they could function at night and during cloudy weather.\n\n## Mechanical Clocks\n\nMechanical clocks first appeared in the 14th century, which were driven by weight and regulated by a verge-and-foliot escapement (a system that advanced the gear train at regular intervals or 'ticks').(no content)Timekeeping took a major step forward in 1283, when the first weight-driven mechanical clock was set in motion in England.\n\n### Pendulum Clocks\nThe invention of the pendulum clock in the 17th century marked another significant advancement in horology. It was Dutch mathematician and scientist Christiaan Huygens who successfully implemented the idea in 1656.(no content)Pendulum clocks were far more accurate than any preceding timekeeping devices.\n\n## Modern Timekeeping\n\n### Quartz Clocks\nThe first quartz clock was built in 1927 by Warren Marrison and Joseph Horton.(no content)These were based on the movement of a vibrating crystal (usually quartz) to create an electric signal with an exact frequency.(no content)Quartz clocks are inexpensive to make and easy to operate, and their invention led to watches and wall-clocks becoming very common from the mid-20th century.\n\n### Atomic Clocks\nIn 1955, NPL set in motion a revolution in timekeeping when Louis Essen and Jack Parry produced the first practical caesium atomic frequency standard.(no content)Atomic clocks use the vibrations of atoms, typically cesium or rubidium, to measure time with extraordinary precision.(no content)Most atomic clocks use the resonance of the atom caesium-133 which vibrates exactly at a frequency of 9,192,631,770 every second. Since 1967 the International System of Units (SI) has defined the second as that number of cycles from this atom.(no content)An atomic clock is accurate to within one second every 100 million years.(no content)These clocks are now the gold standard in timekeeping, and they are used in everything from GPS satellites to scientific research.\n\n## The Future: Optical Clocks\nThe latest innovations, like optical lattice clocks, are outperforming even atomic clocks. Some of these new timers are expected to become mind-bogglingly accurate, losing only one second every 30 billion years."],"durationSeconds":24.058098083}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01K4CMfyxfgPPS3rpjbVuv39","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"atomic clock how it works cesium accuracy\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Beams of Atoms: The First Atomic Clocks | NIST\",\"url\":\"https://www.nist.gov/atomic-clocks/beams-atoms-first-atomic-clocks\"},{\"title\":\"Caesium standard - Wikipedia\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Caesium_standard\"},{\"title\":\"How Does a Practical Cesium Atomic Clock Work? - How Atomic Clocks Work | HowStuffWorks\",\"url\":\"https://science.howstuffworks.com/atomic-clock3.htm\"},{\"title\":\"Cesium clock | instrument | Britannica\",\"url\":\"https://www.britannica.com/technology/cesium-clock\"},{\"title\":\"Atomic clock - Wikipedia\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atomic_clock\"},{\"title\":\"Cesium Atomic Clock\",\"url\":\"http://hyperphysics.phy-astr.gsu.edu/hbase/acloc.html\"},{\"title\":\"Atomic clock | Timekeeping, Accuracy & Benefits | Britannica\",\"url\":\"https://www.britannica.com/technology/atomic-clock\"},{\"title\":\"Atomic Clock: The Atlas of Our Time - USC Viterbi School of Engineering\",\"url\":\"https://illumin.usc.edu/atomic-clock-the-atlas-of-our-time/\"},{\"title\":\"Quantum mechanics - Cesium Clock, Timekeeping, Accuracy | Britannica\",\"url\":\"https://www.britannica.com/science/quantum-mechanics-physics/Cesium-clock\"},{\"title\":\"NIST’s Cesium Fountain Atomic Clocks | NIST\",\"url\":\"https://www.nist.gov/pml/time-and-frequency-division/time-realization/cesium-fountain-atomic-clocks\"}]\n\nHere's what I found about how cesium atomic clocks work and their accuracy:\n\n## How Cesium Atomic Clocks Work\n\n### The Basic Principle\nThe principle underlying the cesium clock is that all atoms of cesium-133 are identical and, when they absorb or release energy, produce radiation of exactly the same frequency, which makes the atoms perfect timepieces.\n\n### The Physics Behind It\nLike all atoms, cesium has a dense nucleus of particles called protons and neutrons, surrounded by a cloud of smaller particles called electrons. One electron wanders far from the rest, on the outskirts of the atom. We use this outermost electron to make a clock.(no content)Quantum physics tells us that every electron is also a tiny magnet that can point in one of two directions. Depending on which direction the electron's magnet is pointing, a cesium atom can have slightly more or less energy. We can make the electron magnet flip by shining light of just the right color, or frequency, on the atom. This causes a quantum jump that sends it from the lower-energy state to the higher one. The special frequency that triggers this jump is called cesium's resonant frequency.\n\n### How It Keeps Time\nTo turn the cesium atomic resonance into an atomic clock, it is necessary to measure one of its transition or resonant frequencies accurately. This is normally done by locking a crystal oscillator to the principal microwave resonance of the cesium atom.(no content)The final step is to count 9,192,631,770 cycles of these finely tuned microwaves. The time it takes to count those cycles is equal to one second.\n\n### The Definition of the Second\nThe official definition of the second was first given by the BIPM at the 13th General Conference on Weights and Measures in 1967 as: \"The second is the duration of 9192631770 periods of the radiation corresponding to the transition between the two hyperfine levels of the ground state of the caesium 133 atom.\"\n\n## Accuracy of Cesium Clocks\n\n### Traditional Cesium Clocks\nThe cesium clock is exceedingly stable. The frequency of the oscillator remains constant to about one part in 10¹³.\n\n### Cesium Fountain Clocks\nThe combination of laser cooling and the fountain design allows the atomic fountain clock to observe cesium atoms for longer periods compared to traditional atomic beam clocks, and thus achieve its unprecedented accuracy.(no content)Laser cooling drops the temperature of the atoms to a few millionths of a degree above absolute zero, and reduces their thermal velocity to a few centimeters per second. The laser cooled atoms are launched vertically and pass twice through a microwave cavity, once on the way up and once on the way down. The result is an observation time of about one second, which is limited only by the force of gravity pulling the atoms to the ground.(no content)The best cesium fountain atomic clocks are now predicted to be off by less than one second in more than 50 million years.\n\n### Progress Over Time\nIn a time period from 1959 to 1998, NIST developed a series of seven caesium-133 microwave clocks named NBS-1 to NBS-6 and NIST-7. The first clock had an accuracy of 10⁻¹¹, and the last clock had an accuracy of 10⁻¹⁵.(no content)Since the first evaluation of NIST-F1, cesium fountain clocks have improved by another order of magnitude and the definition of the second can now be realized with an uncertainty approaching 1×10⁻¹⁶ at metrological institutes around the world.\n\n### Beyond Cesium\nScientists at JILA demonstrated a strontium clock with a frequency precision of 10⁻¹⁸ in 2015. Scientists at NIST developed a quantum logic clock that measured a single aluminum ion in 2019 with a frequency uncertainty of 9.4×10⁻¹⁹.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2854a34c-731f-4835-93ff-d255d2f6ad4c","uuid":"02b44bae-533e-481c-b3f9-8a9c9319bb8b","tool_use_result":{"query":"atomic clock how it works cesium accuracy","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01XZhZ6qFgL1vfETzUxXvy6n","content":[{"title":"Beams of Atoms: The First Atomic Clocks | NIST","url":"https://www.nist.gov/atomic-clocks/beams-atoms-first-atomic-clocks"},{"title":"Caesium standard - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Caesium_standard"},{"title":"How Does a Practical Cesium Atomic Clock Work? - How Atomic Clocks Work | HowStuffWorks","url":"https://science.howstuffworks.com/atomic-clock3.htm"},{"title":"Cesium clock | instrument | Britannica","url":"https://www.britannica.com/technology/cesium-clock"},{"title":"Atomic clock - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atomic_clock"},{"title":"Cesium Atomic Clock","url":"http://hyperphysics.phy-astr.gsu.edu/hbase/acloc.html"},{"title":"Atomic clock | Timekeeping, Accuracy & Benefits | Britannica","url":"https://www.britannica.com/technology/atomic-clock"},{"title":"Atomic Clock: The Atlas of Our Time - USC Viterbi School of Engineering","url":"https://illumin.usc.edu/atomic-clock-the-atlas-of-our-time/"},{"title":"Quantum mechanics - Cesium Clock, Timekeeping, Accuracy | Britannica","url":"https://www.britannica.com/science/quantum-mechanics-physics/Cesium-clock"},{"title":"NIST’s Cesium Fountain Atomic Clocks | NIST","url":"https://www.nist.gov/pml/time-and-frequency-division/time-realization/cesium-fountain-atomic-clocks"}]},"Here's what I found about how cesium atomic clocks work and their accuracy:\n\n## How Cesium Atomic Clocks Work\n\n### The Basic Principle\nThe principle underlying the cesium clock is that all atoms of cesium-133 are identical and, when they absorb or release energy, produce radiation of exactly the same frequency, which makes the atoms perfect timepieces.\n\n### The Physics Behind It\nLike all atoms, cesium has a dense nucleus of particles called protons and neutrons, surrounded by a cloud of smaller particles called electrons. One electron wanders far from the rest, on the outskirts of the atom. We use this outermost electron to make a clock.(no content)Quantum physics tells us that every electron is also a tiny magnet that can point in one of two directions. Depending on which direction the electron's magnet is pointing, a cesium atom can have slightly more or less energy. We can make the electron magnet flip by shining light of just the right color, or frequency, on the atom. This causes a quantum jump that sends it from the lower-energy state to the higher one. The special frequency that triggers this jump is called cesium's resonant frequency.\n\n### How It Keeps Time\nTo turn the cesium atomic resonance into an atomic clock, it is necessary to measure one of its transition or resonant frequencies accurately. This is normally done by locking a crystal oscillator to the principal microwave resonance of the cesium atom.(no content)The final step is to count 9,192,631,770 cycles of these finely tuned microwaves. The time it takes to count those cycles is equal to one second.\n\n### The Definition of the Second\nThe official definition of the second was first given by the BIPM at the 13th General Conference on Weights and Measures in 1967 as: \"The second is the duration of 9192631770 periods of the radiation corresponding to the transition between the two hyperfine levels of the ground state of the caesium 133 atom.\"\n\n## Accuracy of Cesium Clocks\n\n### Traditional Cesium Clocks\nThe cesium clock is exceedingly stable. The frequency of the oscillator remains constant to about one part in 10¹³.\n\n### Cesium Fountain Clocks\nThe combination of laser cooling and the fountain design allows the atomic fountain clock to observe cesium atoms for longer periods compared to traditional atomic beam clocks, and thus achieve its unprecedented accuracy.(no content)Laser cooling drops the temperature of the atoms to a few millionths of a degree above absolute zero, and reduces their thermal velocity to a few centimeters per second. The laser cooled atoms are launched vertically and pass twice through a microwave cavity, once on the way up and once on the way down. The result is an observation time of about one second, which is limited only by the force of gravity pulling the atoms to the ground.(no content)The best cesium fountain atomic clocks are now predicted to be off by less than one second in more than 50 million years.\n\n### Progress Over Time\nIn a time period from 1959 to 1998, NIST developed a series of seven caesium-133 microwave clocks named NBS-1 to NBS-6 and NIST-7. The first clock had an accuracy of 10⁻¹¹, and the last clock had an accuracy of 10⁻¹⁵.(no content)Since the first evaluation of NIST-F1, cesium fountain clocks have improved by another order of magnitude and the definition of the second can now be realized with an uncertainty approaching 1×10⁻¹⁶ at metrological institutes around the world.\n\n### Beyond Cesium\nScientists at JILA demonstrated a strontium clock with a frequency precision of 10⁻¹⁸ in 2015. Scientists at NIST developed a quantum logic clock that measured a single aluminum ion in 2019 with a frequency uncertainty of 9.4×10⁻¹⁹."],"durationSeconds":26.578286208000005}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MTS8u6fgKm4ZxfCK18XWaA","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"how sundials work ancient timekeeping history\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"How did ancient civilizations use sundials to tell time? | HowStuffWorks\",\"url\":\"https://electronics.howstuffworks.com/gadgets/clocks-watches/ancient-civilizations-use-sundials.htm\"},{\"title\":\"Sundials: Tracing the Roots of Timekeeping – Hermle North America\",\"url\":\"https://hermleclock.com/blogs/news/sundials-tracing-the-roots-of-timekeeping\"},{\"title\":\"Sundial | Definition, History, Types, & Facts | Britannica\",\"url\":\"https://www.britannica.com/technology/sundial\"},{\"title\":\"A History Of Sundials: How Shadow Clocks Mark The Passage Of Time - David Harber US\",\"url\":\"https://www.davidharber.com/sundial-history\"},{\"title\":\"History of sundials - Wikipedia\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_sundials\"},{\"title\":\"History of Sundials - First Time Keeping Device\",\"url\":\"https://www.historyofwatch.com/clock-history/history-of-sundials/\"},{\"title\":\"Sundial - Wikipedia\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sundial\"},{\"title\":\"The Evolution of Timekeeping: From Sundials to Smartwatches | WatchCollector.org\",\"url\":\"https://watchcollector.org/2022/04/01/the-evolution-of-timekeeping-from-sundials-to-smartwatches/\"},{\"title\":\"Sundials: Where Time Began - Farmers' Almanac - Plan Your Day. Grow Your Life.\",\"url\":\"https://www.farmersalmanac.com/sundials-time-began\"},{\"title\":\"Sundials | The Engines of Our Ingenuity\",\"url\":\"https://engines.egr.uh.edu/episode/993\"}]\n\nHere's what I found about how sundials work and their history in ancient timekeeping:\n\n## How Sundials Work\n\nA sundial is a device that indicates time by using a light spot or shadow cast by the position of the Sun on a reference scale.(no content)As the Earth turns on its polar axis, the sun appears to cross the sky from east to west, rising at sunrise from beneath the horizon to a zenith at mid-day and falling again behind the horizon at sunset.(no content)In its simplest form, a sundial consists of a flat surface, known as the dial plate, marked with hour lines and a raised arm or stick called the gnomon, which casts a shadow across the dial as the sun moves across the sky.(no content)Ancient sundials used a vertical stick or \"gnomon\" to cast a shadow on a marked surface. As the sun moved across the sky, the shadow moved, indicating the time of day.\n\n## Ancient Origins\n\nThe earliest sundials known from the archaeological record are shadow clocks (1500 BC or BCE) from ancient Egyptian astronomy and Babylonian astronomy.(no content)The first device for indicating the time of day was probably the gnomon, dating from about 3500 BCE.(no content)The oldest known sundial was made in Egypt in 1500 BC. It was L-shaped. The top of its vertical leg cast its shadow on the horizontal leg. The shadow's length, not its location, roughly indicated the time.(no content)The origins of sundials can be traced back to the ancient Egyptians, who first divided the day into segments using simple shadow-casting devices.\n\n## Development Across Civilizations\n\n**Babylonians:** The Babylonians refined sundial techniques, incorporating astronomical observations and advancing the understanding of the azimuth – the sun's angle in relation to true north.\n\n**Greeks:** The ancient Greeks developed many of the principles and forms of the sundial. Sundials are believed to have been introduced into Greece by Anaximander of Miletus, c. 560 BCE.(no content)Another early device was the hemispherical sundial, or hemicycle, attributed to the Greek astronomer Aristarchus of Samos about 280 BCE. Made of stone or wood, the instrument consisted of a cubical block into which a hemispherical opening was cut. To this block a pointer or style was fixed with one end at the centre of the hemispherical space.\n\n**Romans:** Sundials became more advanced in ancient Greece and Rome, where horologists developed a range of different designs to increase their accuracy. The Romans even introduced portable sundials, a predecessor to today's wristwatches.(no content)In 10 BC Emperor Augustus erected the largest Roman sundial Solarium Augusti (30 meters high) that was imported from Egypt.\n\n## Seasonal Hours\n\nThe length and position of the arc varied according to the seasons, so an appropriate number of arcs was inscribed on the internal surface of the hemisphere. Each arc was divided into 12 equal divisions, and each day, reckoned from sunrise to sunset, therefore had 12 equal intervals, or \"hours.\" Because the length of the day varied according to the season, these hours likewise varied in length from season to season and were consequently known as seasonal hours.\n\n## Lasting Legacy\n\nBefore the invention of the clock the sundial was the only way to measure time. After the invention of the clock, the sundial maintained its importance, as clocks needed to be reset regularly from a sundial, because the accuracy of early clocks was poor.(no content)Sundials have been invented independently in every major culture and became more accurate and sophisticated as the culture developed.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2854a34c-731f-4835-93ff-d255d2f6ad4c","uuid":"f2a0ab64-3871-44bb-a584-72981dfc789b","tool_use_result":{"query":"how sundials work ancient timekeeping history","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_014BySas8g6PeHFF5trVxo8t","content":[{"title":"How did ancient civilizations use sundials to tell time? | HowStuffWorks","url":"https://electronics.howstuffworks.com/gadgets/clocks-watches/ancient-civilizations-use-sundials.htm"},{"title":"Sundials: Tracing the Roots of Timekeeping – Hermle North America","url":"https://hermleclock.com/blogs/news/sundials-tracing-the-roots-of-timekeeping"},{"title":"Sundial | Definition, History, Types, & Facts | Britannica","url":"https://www.britannica.com/technology/sundial"},{"title":"A History Of Sundials: How Shadow Clocks Mark The Passage Of Time - David Harber US","url":"https://www.davidharber.com/sundial-history"},{"title":"History of sundials - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_sundials"},{"title":"History of Sundials - First Time Keeping Device","url":"https://www.historyofwatch.com/clock-history/history-of-sundials/"},{"title":"Sundial - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sundial"},{"title":"The Evolution of Timekeeping: From Sundials to Smartwatches | WatchCollector.org","url":"https://watchcollector.org/2022/04/01/the-evolution-of-timekeeping-from-sundials-to-smartwatches/"},{"title":"Sundials: Where Time Began - Farmers' Almanac - Plan Your Day. Grow Your Life.","url":"https://www.farmersalmanac.com/sundials-time-began"},{"title":"Sundials | The Engines of Our Ingenuity","url":"https://engines.egr.uh.edu/episode/993"}]},"Here's what I found about how sundials work and their history in ancient timekeeping:\n\n## How Sundials Work\n\nA sundial is a device that indicates time by using a light spot or shadow cast by the position of the Sun on a reference scale.(no content)As the Earth turns on its polar axis, the sun appears to cross the sky from east to west, rising at sunrise from beneath the horizon to a zenith at mid-day and falling again behind the horizon at sunset.(no content)In its simplest form, a sundial consists of a flat surface, known as the dial plate, marked with hour lines and a raised arm or stick called the gnomon, which casts a shadow across the dial as the sun moves across the sky.(no content)Ancient sundials used a vertical stick or \"gnomon\" to cast a shadow on a marked surface. As the sun moved across the sky, the shadow moved, indicating the time of day.\n\n## Ancient Origins\n\nThe earliest sundials known from the archaeological record are shadow clocks (1500 BC or BCE) from ancient Egyptian astronomy and Babylonian astronomy.(no content)The first device for indicating the time of day was probably the gnomon, dating from about 3500 BCE.(no content)The oldest known sundial was made in Egypt in 1500 BC. It was L-shaped. The top of its vertical leg cast its shadow on the horizontal leg. The shadow's length, not its location, roughly indicated the time.(no content)The origins of sundials can be traced back to the ancient Egyptians, who first divided the day into segments using simple shadow-casting devices.\n\n## Development Across Civilizations\n\n**Babylonians:** The Babylonians refined sundial techniques, incorporating astronomical observations and advancing the understanding of the azimuth – the sun's angle in relation to true north.\n\n**Greeks:** The ancient Greeks developed many of the principles and forms of the sundial. Sundials are believed to have been introduced into Greece by Anaximander of Miletus, c. 560 BCE.(no content)Another early device was the hemispherical sundial, or hemicycle, attributed to the Greek astronomer Aristarchus of Samos about 280 BCE. Made of stone or wood, the instrument consisted of a cubical block into which a hemispherical opening was cut. To this block a pointer or style was fixed with one end at the centre of the hemispherical space.\n\n**Romans:** Sundials became more advanced in ancient Greece and Rome, where horologists developed a range of different designs to increase their accuracy. The Romans even introduced portable sundials, a predecessor to today's wristwatches.(no content)In 10 BC Emperor Augustus erected the largest Roman sundial Solarium Augusti (30 meters high) that was imported from Egypt.\n\n## Seasonal Hours\n\nThe length and position of the arc varied according to the seasons, so an appropriate number of arcs was inscribed on the internal surface of the hemisphere. Each arc was divided into 12 equal divisions, and each day, reckoned from sunrise to sunset, therefore had 12 equal intervals, or \"hours.\" Because the length of the day varied according to the season, these hours likewise varied in length from season to season and were consequently known as seasonal hours.\n\n## Lasting Legacy\n\nBefore the invention of the clock the sundial was the only way to measure time. After the invention of the clock, the sundial maintained its importance, as clocks needed to be reset regularly from a sundial, because the accuracy of early clocks was poor.(no content)Sundials have been invented independently in every major culture and became more accurate and sophisticated as the culture developed."],"durationSeconds":28.871480999999996}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NA83aTg7vkvzdfP8Haw2nA","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"mechanical clock invention history pendulum\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Pendulum clock - Wikipedia\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pendulum_clock\"},{\"title\":\"The invention of the pendulum clock | THE SEIKO MUSEUM GINZA\",\"url\":\"https://museum.seiko.co.jp/en/knowledge/MechanicalTimepieces03/\"},{\"title\":\"History of timekeeping devices - Wikipedia\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_timekeeping_devices\"},{\"title\":\"A Walk Through Time - A Revolution in Timekeeping | NIST\",\"url\":\"https://www.nist.gov/pml/time-and-frequency-division/popular-links/walk-through-time/walk-through-time-revolution\"},{\"title\":\"Huygens Invents the Pendulum Clock, Increasing Accuracy Sixty Fold : History of Information\",\"url\":\"https://www.historyofinformation.com/detail.php?id=3068\"},{\"title\":\"When was the pendulum clock invented? | Britannica\",\"url\":\"https://www.britannica.com/question/When-was-the-pendulum-clock-invented\"},{\"title\":\"The Story of Timekeeping: From Pendulum Clocks to MEMS Oscillators (Part 3 of 3) | SiTime\",\"url\":\"https://www.sitime.com/company/newsroom/blog/story-timekeeping-pendulum-clocks-mems-oscillators-part-3-3\"},{\"title\":\"The Galileo Project | Science | Pendulum Clock\",\"url\":\"https://galileo.library.rice.edu/sci/instruments/pendulum.html\"},{\"title\":\"June 16, 1657: Christiaan Huygens Patents the First Pendulum Clock | American Physical Society\",\"url\":\"https://www.aps.org/publications/apsnews/201706/history.cfm\"},{\"title\":\"Pendulum Clock | COVE\",\"url\":\"https://editions.covecollective.org/chronologies/pendulum-clock\"}]\n\nHere's what I found about the history of the mechanical pendulum clock:\n\n## Invention of the Pendulum Clock\n\nThe pendulum clock was invented on 25 December 1656 by Dutch scientist and inventor Christiaan Huygens, and patented the following year.(no content)He described it in his manuscript Horologium published in 1658. Huygens contracted the construction of his clock designs to the Dutch clockmaker Salomon Coster, who actually built the clock.\n\n## Galileo's Earlier Contributions\n\nHuygens was inspired by investigations of pendulums by Galileo Galilei beginning around 1602. Galileo discovered the key property that makes pendulums useful timekeepers: they are isochronic, which means that the period of swing of a pendulum is approximately the same for different sized swings.(no content)Galileo Galilei is credited with inventing the pendulum-clock concept, and he studied the motion of the pendulum as early as 1582. He even sketched out a design for a pendulum clock, but he never actually constructed one before his death in 1642.\n\n## Accuracy Improvements\n\nThis technology reduced the loss of time by clocks from about 15 minutes to about 15 seconds per day.(no content)Huygens' early pendulum clock had an error of less than 1 minute a day, the first time such accuracy had been achieved. His later refinements reduced his clock's error to less than 10 seconds a day.\n\n## Earlier Mechanical Clocks\n\nThe foliot balance served as a regulator from the time that the first mechanical clocks were built as tower clocks during the Renaissance in around 1300, until it was superseded by the isochronous pendulum.(no content)Mechanical clocks were a major breakthrough, one notably designed and built by Henry de Vick in c. 1360, which established basic clock design for the next 300 years. Minor developments were added, such as the invention of the mainspring in the early 15th century, which allowed small clocks to be built for the first time.\n\n## Further Developments\n\nIn London in 1671, William Clement began building clocks with the new \"anchor\" or \"recoil\" escapement, a substantial improvement over the verge because it interferes less with the motion of the pendulum.(no content)Clockmakers' realization that only pendulums with small swings of a few degrees are isochronous motivated the invention of the anchor escapement around 1670, which reduced the pendulum's swing to 4°-6°. The anchor became the standard escapement used in pendulum clocks.\n\n## Legacy\n\nFrom its invention in 1656 by Christiaan Huygens, inspired by Galileo Galilei, until the 1930s, the pendulum clock was the world's most precise timekeeper, accounting for its widespread use. Throughout the 18th and 19th centuries, pendulum clocks in homes, factories, offices, and railroad stations served as primary time standards for scheduling daily life activities, work shifts, and public transportation. Their greater accuracy allowed for a faster pace of life which was necessary for the Industrial Revolution.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2854a34c-731f-4835-93ff-d255d2f6ad4c","uuid":"0ed8c2d6-5bca-4a47-813a-174f848c99e7","tool_use_result":{"query":"mechanical clock invention history pendulum","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01LoZW9yKHf5CidPmFNxVv5H","content":[{"title":"Pendulum clock - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pendulum_clock"},{"title":"The invention of the pendulum clock | THE SEIKO MUSEUM GINZA","url":"https://museum.seiko.co.jp/en/knowledge/MechanicalTimepieces03/"},{"title":"History of timekeeping devices - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_timekeeping_devices"},{"title":"A Walk Through Time - A Revolution in Timekeeping | NIST","url":"https://www.nist.gov/pml/time-and-frequency-division/popular-links/walk-through-time/walk-through-time-revolution"},{"title":"Huygens Invents the Pendulum Clock, Increasing Accuracy Sixty Fold : History of Information","url":"https://www.historyofinformation.com/detail.php?id=3068"},{"title":"When was the pendulum clock invented? | Britannica","url":"https://www.britannica.com/question/When-was-the-pendulum-clock-invented"},{"title":"The Story of Timekeeping: From Pendulum Clocks to MEMS Oscillators (Part 3 of 3) | SiTime","url":"https://www.sitime.com/company/newsroom/blog/story-timekeeping-pendulum-clocks-mems-oscillators-part-3-3"},{"title":"The Galileo Project | Science | Pendulum Clock","url":"https://galileo.library.rice.edu/sci/instruments/pendulum.html"},{"title":"June 16, 1657: Christiaan Huygens Patents the First Pendulum Clock | American Physical Society","url":"https://www.aps.org/publications/apsnews/201706/history.cfm"},{"title":"Pendulum Clock | COVE","url":"https://editions.covecollective.org/chronologies/pendulum-clock"}]},"Here's what I found about the history of the mechanical pendulum clock:\n\n## Invention of the Pendulum Clock\n\nThe pendulum clock was invented on 25 December 1656 by Dutch scientist and inventor Christiaan Huygens, and patented the following year.(no content)He described it in his manuscript Horologium published in 1658. Huygens contracted the construction of his clock designs to the Dutch clockmaker Salomon Coster, who actually built the clock.\n\n## Galileo's Earlier Contributions\n\nHuygens was inspired by investigations of pendulums by Galileo Galilei beginning around 1602. Galileo discovered the key property that makes pendulums useful timekeepers: they are isochronic, which means that the period of swing of a pendulum is approximately the same for different sized swings.(no content)Galileo Galilei is credited with inventing the pendulum-clock concept, and he studied the motion of the pendulum as early as 1582. He even sketched out a design for a pendulum clock, but he never actually constructed one before his death in 1642.\n\n## Accuracy Improvements\n\nThis technology reduced the loss of time by clocks from about 15 minutes to about 15 seconds per day.(no content)Huygens' early pendulum clock had an error of less than 1 minute a day, the first time such accuracy had been achieved. His later refinements reduced his clock's error to less than 10 seconds a day.\n\n## Earlier Mechanical Clocks\n\nThe foliot balance served as a regulator from the time that the first mechanical clocks were built as tower clocks during the Renaissance in around 1300, until it was superseded by the isochronous pendulum.(no content)Mechanical clocks were a major breakthrough, one notably designed and built by Henry de Vick in c. 1360, which established basic clock design for the next 300 years. Minor developments were added, such as the invention of the mainspring in the early 15th century, which allowed small clocks to be built for the first time.\n\n## Further Developments\n\nIn London in 1671, William Clement began building clocks with the new \"anchor\" or \"recoil\" escapement, a substantial improvement over the verge because it interferes less with the motion of the pendulum.(no content)Clockmakers' realization that only pendulums with small swings of a few degrees are isochronous motivated the invention of the anchor escapement around 1670, which reduced the pendulum's swing to 4°-6°. The anchor became the standard escapement used in pendulum clocks.\n\n## Legacy\n\nFrom its invention in 1656 by Christiaan Huygens, inspired by Galileo Galilei, until the 1930s, the pendulum clock was the world's most precise timekeeper, accounting for its widespread use. Throughout the 18th and 19th centuries, pendulum clocks in homes, factories, offices, and railroad stations served as primary time standards for scheduling daily life activities, work shifts, and public transportation. Their greater accuracy allowed for a faster pace of life which was necessary for the Industrial Revolution."],"durationSeconds":29.964116916}}
